+The @i{PLUGIN_EXPLICIT_INSTANTIATION} event is triggered by the C++ front
+end for each explicit instantiation directive that it parses with no
+error, after it does the instantiation.  The GCC data is a pointer to a
+@code{struct plugin_explicit_instantiation_data}, declared in
+@file{cp/cp-tree.h}.  Its field @code{entity} is the type that the
+directive instantiates, or the declaration of the function or variable
+that it instantiates.  Its field @code{location} is the location of the
+first token of the directive, and its field @code{extern_p} is true for
+an explicit instantiation declaration.  A plugin cannot find this
+information later: at the end of the translation unit, an instantiated
+class has the location of its template, and no list of the front end
+holds the directive.
+
+The @i{PLUGIN_SIZEOF_OR_ALIGNOF} event is triggered by the C++ front end
+for each @code{sizeof} or @code{alignof} expression that it parses with
+no error, also in a template.  The GCC data is a pointer to a
+@code{struct plugin_sizeof_or_alignof_data}, declared in
+@file{cp/cp-tree.h}.  Its field @code{operand} is the operand as the
+parser read it, a type or an expression.  Its field @code{location} is
+the location of the expression, and its field @code{code} is
+@code{SIZEOF_EXPR} or @code{ALIGNOF_EXPR}.  The event comes before the
+front end folds the expression to a constant.  The parser can read one
+expression more than one time when it parses tentatively, so a plugin can
+see one operator more than one time.  The event does not come for the
+instantiation of a template.
+
+The @i{PLUGIN_NAME_USE} event is triggered by the C++ front end each time
+that its parser resolves a name to a declaration: an id-expression, a
+qualified name, a type name, a template name or a namespace name.  It is
+a direct event (see below), and its callback has the type
+@code{plugin_name_use_func}, declared in @file{plugin.h}:
+
+@smallexample
+typedef void (*plugin_name_use_func) (tree decl, location_t location,
+                                      void *user_data);
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+The argument @code{decl} is the declaration that the name resolves to,
+or the @code{OVERLOAD} or @code{BASELINK} of a set of overloaded
+functions.  The argument @code{location} is the location of the name.
+For a qualified name, it is the location of the last name, or a range
+from the first name to the last name.  A dependent name gives no event.
+The event comes before any
+constant folding, so a plugin sees each
+name of a constant expression, of a @code{static_assert}, of a template
+argument and of an unevaluated operand.  The name of a member after
+@code{.} or @code{->} is resolved later, so it gives no event, and the
+@i{PLUGIN_MEMBER_ACCESS} event gives that member.  The parser can read one
+name more than one time when it parses tentatively, so a plugin can see
+one use more than one time.  The event does not come for the instantiation
+of a template.
+
+The @i{PLUGIN_USING_FORM} event is triggered by the C++ front end for each
+using-declaration, using-directive, namespace alias and alias declaration
+that it parses with no error, at each scope.  The GCC data is a pointer to
+a @code{struct plugin_using_form_data}, declared in @file{cp/cp-tree.h}.
+Its field @code{kind} names the form.  Its field @code{decl} is what the
+form declares: the @code{USING_DECL} of a using-declaration, the
+@code{NAMESPACE_DECL} of a namespace alias or the @code{TYPE_DECL} of an
+alias declaration, and it is null for a using-directive.  Its field
+@code{target} is what the form names: the declarations that a
+using-declaration names, the namespace that a using-directive nominates or
+that a namespace alias names, or the type that an alias declaration names.
+Its field @code{location} is the location of the name that the form names
+or declares.  A using-declaration with a list of names gives one event for
+each name.
+
+The @i{PLUGIN_MEMBER_ACCESS} event is triggered by the C++ front end each
+time that it resolves the member of a class member access expression with
+@code{.} or @code{->}, also a member that a splice designates.  It is a
+direct event (see below), and its callback has the type
+@code{plugin_member_access_func}, declared in @file{plugin.h}:
+
+@smallexample
+typedef void (*plugin_member_access_func) (tree member, tree object_type,
+                                           location_t location,
+                                           bool splice_p, void *user_data);
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+The argument @code{member} is the member: a @code{FIELD_DECL}, a
+@code{VAR_DECL}, the @code{BASELINK} of a set of member functions, or a
+@code{TEMPLATE_ID_EXPR} for a member template.  The argument
+@code{object_type} is the class type of the object.  The argument
+@code{location} is @code{input_location} when the front end resolves the
+member; at parse time it is the last token of the member name.  The
+argument @code{splice_p} is true when a splice designates the member.  The
+event
+comes at parse time and again at the instantiation of a template, so a
+plugin can see one access more than one time.  A member access that the
+front end makes itself, as for a range-based @code{for} or a structured
+binding, gives the event too.
+
+The @i{PLUGIN_REFLECTION} event is triggered by the C++ front end for each
+reflect-expression (@code{^^X}) that its parser parses with no error, and
+each time that it resolves a splice-specifier (@code{[:R:]}).  The GCC
+data is a pointer to a @code{struct plugin_reflection_data}, declared in
+@file{cp/cp-tree.h}.  Its field @code{kind} is
+@code{PLUGIN_REFLECT_OPERATOR} or @code{PLUGIN_SPLICE}.  Its field
+@code{entity} is the entity that the reflection represents or that the
+splice designates.  Its field @code{location} is the location of the
+operand of the reflect-expression, or @code{input_location} when the
+front end resolves the splice.  A splice resolves at parse time, or at the
+instantiation of a template when it is dependent.  The parser can read one
+expression more than one time when it parses tentatively, so a plugin can
+see one reflection more than one time.  A name in the operand of a
+reflect-expression also gives the @i{PLUGIN_NAME_USE} event, so a plugin
+can tell a reflection of a name from a plain use of it.
+
+The @i{PLUGIN_NAME_USE} and @i{PLUGIN_MEMBER_ACCESS} events are direct
+events.  They occur very often, so the front end calls their callbacks
+itself, with the facts of the event as arguments, and not through
+@code{invoke_plugin_callbacks}.  A plugin registers such a callback with
+@code{register_callback}, as for each other event, and casts it to
+@code{plugin_callback_func}.  Cast it through @code{void (*) (void)} to
+prevent the warning of @option{-Wcast-function-type}:
+
+@smallexample
+static void
+name_use (tree decl, location_t location, void *user_data)
+@{
+  @dots{}
+@}
+
+register_callback (plugin_info->base_name, PLUGIN_NAME_USE,
+                   (plugin_callback_func) (void (*) (void)) name_use,
+                   NULL);
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+Each direct event has a slot, declared in @file{plugin.h}, that holds the
+function that the front end calls and its user data.  When one callback
+reads the event, the slot holds that callback, so the front end calls it
+with no other work.  When more than one callback reads the event, the
+slot holds a function of GCC that calls each callback.  The time of a
+direct callback is part of the time of the front end, and not of the
+timer of the plugins.  When no plugin registered a callback for an event,
+@code{invoke_plugin_callbacks_full} returns before it starts the timer of
+the plugins.
+
 The @i{PLUGIN_FINISH} event is the last time that plugins can call GCC
 functions, notably emit diagnostics with @code{warning}, @code{error}
 etc.
